FunnyLab.cards =E2=80=94 Coding Integration Addendum

This updates the earlier plan. Keep the first build simple.

CORE PRODUCT

FunnyLab.cards is the AI/AGI card product.
FunnyLab is the broader comedy/social brand.
Muse or ChatGPT is the interface.
Jev is the fast routing/ranking layer.
A generative model writes candidate jokes and image briefs.
Prodigi handles physical fulfilment.
Etsy handles initial discovery and checkout.

User experience:

"Make James a dry Christmas card about his AGI obsession. Use my dog."

Muse supplies only the relevant context summary.

FunnyLab:
1. generates several joke candidates;
2. uses Jev to rank candidate + template + tone fit;
3. renders a preview;
4. returns product/shipping quote;
5. waits for user approval;
6. sends approved print assets to Prodigi.


FIRST NICHE

Do not launch as generic cards.

Initial collection:
AI / AGI comedy, especially Christmas.

Positioning:
"Funny cards for the AI age."

Themes:
- automation
- robots
- work and redundancy
- future anxiety
- AI relationships
- pets reacting to AI
- Christmas + AI


HERO CARD

Scene:
Santa in a warm Christmas setting reading a printed notice.

Notice:
"Merry Christmas!
Your role has been automated."

Potential inside copy:
"Please do not reply to this message. Seasonal decisions are final."

Alternative:
"Hope your festive period remains at least partially human."

Personalized:
"Merry Christmas, James.
Your role has been automated."

This is the benchmark visual/tone for the first collection.


FIRST TEN AI CHRISTMAS TEMPLATE FAMILIES

1. Santa automated
"Merry Christmas! Your role has been automated."

2. Santa vs robots / breaking news
"BREAKING: 10 million robots taken offline in unexplained sleigh-based atta=
ck."

3. Elf redundancy notice
"North Pole announces restructuring following agent deployment."

4. Pet security failure
"STRANGER ENTERED HOME VIA CHIMNEY.
DOG ACCEPTED BISCUIT.
INVESTIGATION CLOSED."

5. Christmas performance review
Turkey: adequate
Walks: unacceptable
Wrapping: embarrassing
Guest selection: questionable

6. Human vs pet
Human: "Will Christmas still feel human after AGI?"
Pet: "Turkey."

7. Cat vs Christmas tree
"Decorative structure destroyed 14 minutes after deployment."

8. Santa reads owner search history
Santa horrified, pet smug.

9. AI Christmas card
Human: "Will Christmas still feel human after AGI?"
Pet: "You bought this card from an AI."

10. Pet North Pole complaint
"Requesting replacement owner. Current unit misses scheduled feeding window=
s."


TEMPLATE-FIRST ARCHITECTURE

The unit of creation is a comedy template, not an Etsy SKU.

One template should support:
- physical card
- digital card
- shirt
- social image
- later motion/video

Template schema:

{
  id,
  family,
  required_inputs,
  optional_inputs,
  text_slots,
  image_slots,
  humour_modes,
  supported_products,
  supported_occasions,
  topical_tags,
  print_layout,
  digital_layout
}


HUMOUR MODES

Support at least:
- dry
- absurd
- deadpan
- corporate
- topical
- sharper roast

Do not merely rewrite one joke five times.
Generate structurally different candidates.


JEV ROLE

Jev should not write the joke.

Use it for typed decisions after candidate generation.

Input state:
{
  humour_context,
  recipient_summary,
  occasion,
  available_templates,
  candidate_jokes,
  topical_context,
  product_type
}

Questions:
- best_template
- best_candidate
- tone_fit
- personal_fit
- instant_clarity
- genericness
- topical_fit
- whether more context is needed

Rules:
- high confidence: route automatically
- low confidence: show multiple options
- high genericness: regenerate
- low instant clarity: regenerate

Jev is the cheap routing/ranking layer.


MUSE CONTEXT

Accept a minimized context object such as:

{
  preferred_tones: ["dry","absurd"],
  disliked_tones: ["sentimental"],
  recurring_topics: ["AGI"],
  relevant_in_jokes: [],
  avoid_topics: [],
  recipient_relevant_facts: []
}

Do not pass raw conversation history.
Only use relevant user-provided or permitted context.


MCP SURFACE

Keep it small:

funny.list_templates()

funny.create_concepts({
  recipient,
  occasion,
  context_summary,
  humour_mode,
  topical_context?,
  photo?
})

funny.preview({
  template_id,
  copy,
  photo?
})

funny.quote({
  product,
  destination
})

funny.order({
  approved_preview_id,
  destination,
  shipping_option
})

funny.create_card({
  recipient,
  occasion,
  context_summary,
  humour_mode,
  photo?,
  topical_context?,
  destination?
})

create_card flow:
generate
=E2=86=92 Jev rank
=E2=86=92 template selection
=E2=86=92 render preview
=E2=86=92 quote
=E2=86=92 return preview

Do not place a paid order until explicit approval.


STATIC RENDERING

Prefer:
Satori =E2=86=92 SVG
resvg-js =E2=86=92 high-resolution PNG
Sharp =E2=86=92 photo crop/composite/resize/print preparation

Each card template should live as a version-controlled component.

Example:

<AutomatedSantaCard
  recipient=3D"James"
  headline=3D"Merry Christmas!"
  body=3D"Your role has been automated."
  humourMode=3D"dry"
/>

Use Creatomate later for animated/social versions.


PRODUCT TIERS

STATIC
fixed joke

PERSONALIZED
name/photo/context substitution

WILDCARD
context-aware custom joke + template selection

All use the same template library.


FEEDBACK LOOP

Store raw outcomes:

template_id
candidate_id
tone
topic
occasion
personalization_depth
Jev outputs
selected?
rewrite requested?
purchased?
shared?
social performance?

Do not collapse this into one "funny score" initially.


FUNNYLABS =E2=80=94 AFTER LAUNCH

Do not let this block the store.

Reuse useful /killella concepts:

Comedian
=E2=86=92 immutable ActVersion
=E2=86=92 Appearance
=E2=86=92 performance/reaction data
=E2=86=92 next version

Controlled experiments:

A. same joke, different character
B. same character, different joke structure
C. same material, different delivery
D. same template, different personalization depth
E. same structure, topical vs evergreen

Important thesis:
humour depends on more than text.

Eventually track:

material
=C3=97 speaker
=C3=97 character appearance
=C3=97 voice
=C3=97 delivery
=C3=97 timing
=C3=97 context
=C3=97 audience
=C3=97 medium
=E2=86=92 response

We are not trying to solve humour in advance.
We are building the content + commerce + measurement system so better
humour models can be swapped in later.


SOCIAL LOOP

Winning social idea:
=E2=86=92 static card
=E2=86=92 personalized card
=E2=86=92 shirt/digital version

Winning commerce format:
=E2=86=92 social sketch or recurring character bit

FunnyLab becomes the social/comedy brand.
FunnyLab.cards is the commercial card surface.


Q4 BUILD ORDER

1. Design 10 AI/AGI Christmas cards manually.
2. Make the art excellent.
3. Build static renderer.
4. Put cards live on Etsy.
5. Wire Prodigi.
6. Add personalization.
7. Expose MCP.
8. Add Jev routing/ranking.
9. Log feedback.
10. Start FunnyLabs experiments after launch.

SUCCESS CONDITION

A user can say:

"Make Jake a dry Christmas card about AGI replacing him. Use my dog."

Without leaving Muse:
- FunnyLab chooses a template
- generates candidate copy
- Jev ranks it
- card is rendered
- preview is shown
- shipping is quoted
- user approves
- Prodigi fulfils

CORE RULE

Do not make the technology interesting before the jokes are interesting.

The first real asset is:
10 genuinely funny reusable AI/AGI card templates
+ strong personalization
+ reliable fulfilment
+ feedback on what people actually choose and buy.
